  ("maximum number of stimulus presentations all influence the magnitude of the exposure effect." 0.2092 0.7897 0.2728 0.2874 0.2874 0.0146 0.00383 0.3038 0.0609 "Times-Roman" 0.0111 nil nil 0)
  ("Implications of these findings are discussed in the context of previous reviews of the literature on" 0.2092 0.7892 0.2855 0.3000 0.3000 0.0146 0.00387 0.2824 0.0745 "Times-Roman" 0.0112 nil nil 0)
  ("exposure effects and with respect to prevailing theoretical models of the exposure-affect relationship." 0.2087 0.7899 0.2984 0.3130 0.3130 0.0146 0.00391 0.3261 0.0522 "Times-Roman" 0.0114 nil nil 0)
  ("Modifications of the 2-factor model of exposure effects that increase the heuristic value of the model" 0.2096 0.7903 0.3111 0.3256 0.3256 0.0146 0.00384 0.3409 0.0811 "Times-Roman" 0.0112 nil nil 0)
  ("are described. A possible evolutionary basis of the exposure effect is discussed." 0.2096 0.6665 0.3240 0.3386 0.3386 0.0146 0.00391 0.2915 0.0180 "Times-Roman" 0.0112 nil nil 0)
  ("Psychologists have long observed that repeated, unreinforced" 0.0850 0.4841 0.3751 0.3914 0.3914 0.0163 0.00437 0.2712 0.0875 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("exposure results in an increase in positive affect toward a stimu-" 0.0696 0.4849 0.3890 0.4053 0.4053 0.0163 0.00437 0.3383 0.0605 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("lus (Fechner, 1876; Maslow, 1937). A number of early studies" 0.0696 0.4856 0.4029 0.4192 0.4192 0.0163 0.00437 0.3242 0.0189 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("in this area (e.g., Meyer, 1903; Mpore & Gilliland, 1924; Wash-" 0.0696 0.4853 0.4171 0.4334 0.4334 0.0163 0.00437 0.4321 0.0126 "Times-Roman" 0.0123 nil nil 0)
  ("burn, Child, & Abel, 1927) investigated the effects of repeated" 0.0696 0.4849 0.4313 0.4475 0.4475 0.0163 0.00427 0.3128 0.0367 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("exposures on affect ratings of music, generally finding more" 0.0700 0.4855 0.4451 0.4614 0.4614 0.0163 0.00437 0.2662 0.0653 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("positive ratings with increasing familiarity. Many of these ex-" 0.0696 0.4853 0.4590 0.4753 0.4753 0.0163 0.00437 0.3477 0.0507 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("periments were methodologically unsound, however (i.e., in-" 0.0696 0.4853 0.4729 0.4892 0.4892 0.0163 0.00437 0.3082 0.0654 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("volved improper control conditions or groups or no controls at" 0.0700 0.4850 0.4871 0.5034 0.5034 0.0163 0.00437 0.2759 0.0433 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("all), and conclusions based on their results are somewhat tenu-" 0.0696 0.4848 0.5010 0.5173 0.5173 0.0163 0.00437 0.2976 0.0257 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("ous. Additional indirect evidence for the hypothesis that in-" 0.0700 0.4857 0.5152 0.5314 0.5314 0.0163 0.00437 0.2413 0.0269 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("creased exposure leads to more positive affect toward a stimulus" 0.0700 0.4860 0.5293 0.5456 0.5456 0.0163 0.00437 0.3561 0.0478 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("came from studies of social interaction (e.g., Festinger, 1951;" 0.0700 0.4861 0.5429 0.5592 0.5592 0.0163 0.00437 0.2807 0.0355 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("Homans, 1961;Newcomb, 1963). Although these investigations" 0.0704 0.4856 0.5571 0.5734 0.5734 0.0163 0.00437 0.3547 0.0596 "Times-Roman" 0.0127 nil nil 0)
  ("consistently demonstrated that interaction enhances the atti-" 0.0700 0.4857 0.5713 0.5876 0.5876 0.0163 0.00437 0.2579 0.0767 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("tudes of participants toward each other, it is impossible to tell" 0.0696 0.4849 0.5852 0.6015 0.6015 0.0163 0.00437 0.3039 0.0351 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("to what degree this is due to mere exposure, to reinforcement" 0.0700 0.4854 0.5994 0.6157 0.6157 0.0163 0.00437 0.3153 0.0134 "Times-Roman" 0.0127 nil nil 0)
  ("derived from social interaction, or to some combination of these" 0.0704 0.4851 0.6133 0.6296 0.6296 0.0163 0.00437 0.3461 0.0479 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("(and other) variables." 0.0704 0.2104 0.6274 0.6437 0.6437 0.0163 0.00437 0.2442 0.0295 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("With the publication of Zajonc's (1968) monograph on the" 0.0858 0.4855 0.6413 0.6576 0.6576 0.0163 0.00434 0.3130 0.0334 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("\"Attitudinal Effects of Mere Exposure,\" the exposure effect be-" 0.0704 0.4861 0.6552 0.6715 0.6715 0.0163 0.00425 0.3204 0.0820 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("came a topic of investigation in mainstream psychology. Zajonc" 0.0704 0.4895 0.6694 0.6857 0.6857 0.0163 0.00437 0.3536 0.0348 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("(1968) defined the exposure effect as the observation that \"mere" 0.0704 0.4855 0.6833 0.6996 0.6996 0.0163 0.00433 0.3058 0.0436 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("repeated exposure of the individual to a stimulus is a sufficient" 0.0708 0.4854 0.6975 0.7138 0.7138 0.0163 0.00439 0.3031 0.0552 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("condition for the enhancement of his attitude toward it. By" 0.0708 0.4855 0.7117 0.7280 0.7280 0.0163 0.0043 0.2809 0.0622 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("'mere exposure' is meant a condition which just makes the given" 0.0704 0.4847 0.7256 0.7418 0.7418 0.0163 0.00437 0.4233 0.0380 "Times-Roman" 0.0129 nil nil 0)
  ("stimulus accessible to the individual's perception\" (p. 1). Za-" 0.0704 0.4857 0.7397 0.7560 0.7560 0.0163 0.00442 0.2889 0.0573 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("jonc thus suggested that simple, unreinforced exposure leads" 0.0688 0.4860 0.7536 0.7699 0.7699 0.0163 0.00437 0.2452 0.0294 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("I would like to thank Nancy E. Cantor, Paul R. D'Agostino, Mary A." 0.0842 0.4852 0.7982 0.8127 0.8127 0.0146 0.00391 0.4426 0.0049 "Times-Roman" 0.0109 nil nil 0)
  ("Languirand, Thane S. Pittman and two anonymous reviewers for their" 0.0708 0.4837 0.8108 0.8254 0.8254 0.0146 0.00386 0.3440 0.0716 "Times-Roman" 0.0111 nil nil 0)
  ("helpful comments on earlier versions of the manuscript. I would also" 0.0708 0.4847 0.8238 0.8383 0.8383 0.0146 0.00391 0.3205 0.0393 "Times-Roman" 0.0112 nil nil 0)
  ("like to thank Amy R. Kale and Barbara J. Hardy for help in conducting" 0.0708 0.4853 0.8364 0.8510 0.8510 0.0146 0.00379 0.3939 0.0208 "Times-Roman" 0.0112 nil nil 0)
  ("literature searches for the meta-analysis." 0.0704 0.3044 0.8491 0.8636 0.8636 0.0146 0.00391 0.2952 0.0546 "Times-Roman" 0.0112 nil nil 0)
  ("Correspondence concerning this article should be addressed to Rob-" 0.0842 0.4852 0.8617 0.8763 0.8763 0.0146 0.00391 0.2555 0.0935 "Times-Roman" 0.0112 nil nil 0)
  ("ert F. Bornstein, Department of Psychology, Gettysburg College, Get-" 0.0704 0.4852 0.8744 0.8889 0.8889 0.0146 0.00391 0.3286 0.0151 "Times-Roman" 0.0111 nil nil 0)
  ("tysburg, Pennsylvania 17325." 0.0704 0.2466 0.8870 0.9016 0.9016 0.0146 0.00391 0.2297 0.0474 "Times-Roman" 0.0111 nil nil 0)
  ("to increased liking for a stimulus; in short, familiarity leads to" 0.5162 0.9314 0.3748 0.3911 0.3911 0.0163 0.00437 0.3336 0.0132 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("liking." 0.5162 0.5584 0.3887 0.4050 0.4050 0.0163 nil 0.3078 0.0422 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("Empirical and correlational evidence cited by Zajonc (1968)" 0.5321 0.9315 0.4038 0.4201 0.4201 0.0163 0.00437 0.3052 0.0656 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("in support of this hypothesis delineated what became the proto" 0.5162 0.9315 0.4180 0.4343 0.4343 0.0163 0.00437 0.3415 0.0127 "Times-Roman" 0.0123 nil nil 0)
  ("typic methodologies used by other researchers to investigate the" 0.5162 0.9317 0.4322 0.4485 0.4485 0.0163 0.00437 0.3027 0.0335 "Times-Roman" 0.0127 nil nil 0)
  ("exposure-affect relationship. The basic design of most labora-" 0.5167 0.9319 0.4461 0.4623 0.4623 0.0163 0.00437 0.2576 0.1019 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("tory mere exposure research is derived from Zajonc's (1968)" 0.5162 0.9315 0.4602 0.4765 0.4765 0.0163 0.00437 0.2554 0.0272 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("Experiments 1-3, and the general approach used by later re-" 0.5167 0.9319 0.4744 0.4907 0.4907 0.0163 0.00437 0.2399 0.0844 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("searchers to examine the exposure-affect relationship in vivo" 0.5167 0.9318 0.4886 0.5049 0.5049 0.0163 0.00437 0.2451 0.0594 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("(i.e., in \"naturalistic\" studies of exposure and affect) is derived" 0.5162 0.9312 0.5028 0.5191 0.5191 0.0163 0.00434 0.2742 0.0282 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("from the correlational evidence cited by Zajonc. Thus, the typi-" 0.5167 0.9315 0.5170 0.5333 0.5333 0.0163 0.00437 0.3183 0.0307 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("cal laboratory study of exposure and affect involves presenting" 0.5167 0.9320 0.5312 0.5475 0.5475 0.0163 0.00437 0.2528 0.0191 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("varying numbers of exposures of neutral visual stimuli (e.g.," 0.5171 0.9313 0.5451 0.5614 0.5614 0.0163 0.00437 0.2775 0.0499 "Times-Roman" 0.0123 nil nil 0)
  ("line drawings, nonsense words), after which some dependent" 0.5167 0.9312 0.5596 0.5759 0.5759 0.0163 0.00437 0.2678 0.0251 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("measure of affect regarding each stimulus is collected. Natural-" 0.5171 0.9319 0.5735 0.5898 0.5898 0.0163 0.00437 0.3307 0.0546 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("istic studies, which focus on stimuli present in the natural envi-" 0.5167 0.9315 0.5877 0.6039 0.6039 0.0163 0.00437 0.3366 0.0268 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("ronment (e.g., names of public figures, common items), obtain" 0.5167 0.9313 0.6018 0.6181 0.6181 0.0163 0.00437 0.3827 0.0570 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("subjects' affect ratings of these stimuli using procedures similar" 0.5171 0.9320 0.6160 0.6323 0.6323 0.0163 0.00437 0.3288 0.0553 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("to those used in laboratory mere exposure studies but with fre-" 0.5167 0.9324 0.6302 0.6465 0.6465 0.0163 0.00437 0.3086 0.0132 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("quency of exposure estimated via some unobtrusive index such" 0.5167 0.9309 0.6444 0.6607 0.6607 0.0163 0.00437 0.3074 0.0476 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("as the Thorndike and Lorge (1944) word frequency count or" 0.5167 0.9316 0.6586 0.6749 0.6749 0.0163 0.00432 0.2120 0.0140 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("subjects' self-reports. Both laboratory and naturalistic studies" 0.5171 0.9323 0.6728 0.6891 0.6891 0.0163 0.00437 0.2597 0.0556 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("of the exposure effect generally utilize within-subjects designs," 0.5171 0.9317 0.6870 0.7033 0.7033 0.0163 0.00437 0.2721 0.0134 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("with each subject providing ratings of a number of stimuli that" 0.5167 0.9312 0.7012 0.7175 0.7175 0.0163 0.00437 0.3483 0.0297 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("differ in amount of previous exposure or familiarity." 0.5171 0.8604 0.7154 0.7317 0.7317 0.0163 0.00437 0.3488 0.0330 "Times-Roman" 0.0123 nil nil 0)
  ("Zajonc's (1968) initial correlational evidence indicated that" 0.5325 0.9312 0.7302 0.7465 0.7465 0.0163 0.00437 0.2559 0.0551 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("relationships exist between the Thorndike and Lorge (1944)" 0.5171 0.9319 0.7444 0.7607 0.7607 0.0163 0.00437 0.2304 0.0839 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("word frequency count and the evaluative meaning of a word, as" 0.5167 0.9314 0.7586 0.7749 0.7749 0.0163 0.00431 0.3399 0.0333 "Times-Roman" 0.0123 nil nil 0)
  ("well as between word frequency and attitudes toward the object" 0.5167 0.9312 0.7728 0.7890 0.7890 0.0163 0.00437 0.3343 0.0266 "Times-Roman" 0.0122 nil nil 0)
  ("described by a word. Presumably, these relationships reflect at-" 0.5167 0.9319 0.7869 0.8032 0.8032 0.0163 0.00437 0.3029 0.0613 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("titude enhancement resulting from repeated exposure to partic-" 0.5162 0.9320 0.8011 0.8174 0.8174 0.0163 0.00437 0.3109 0.0381 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("ular words. Because these data are correlational, though, it is" 0.5171 0.9323 0.8153 0.8316 0.8316 0.0163 0.00437 0.2598 0.0261 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("impossible to demonstrate any causal relationship between" 0.5171 0.9313 0.8292 0.8455 0.8455 0.0163 0.00437 0.2824 0.0707 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("word frequency and evaluative meaning or attitude change." 0.5167 0.9316 0.8434 0.8597 0.8597 0.0163 0.00437 0.2334 0.0333 "Times-Roman" 0.0123 nil nil 0)
  ("However, Zajonc also presented a series of experiments testing" 0.5171 0.9316 0.8579 0.8742 0.8742 0.0163 0.00437 0.3013 0.0596 "Times-Roman" 0.0122 nil nil 0)
  ("his mere exposure hypothesis. Stronger evidence for the expo-" 0.5171 0.9324 0.8721 0.8884 0.8884 0.0163 0.00437 0.2521 0.0189 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("sure-affect relationship comes from these studies." 0.5167 0.8433 0.8863 0.9026 0.9026 0.0163 0.00437 0.3066 0.0698 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("265" 0.4904 0.5127 0.9227 0.9390 0.9390 0.0163 nil 0.0000 0.0222 "Times-Roman" 0.0117 nil nil 0))
  (2
  ("266" 0.0675 0.0949 0.0534 0.0732 0.0732 0.0197 nil 0.0000 0.0274 "Times-Roman" 0.0144 nil nil 0)
  ("ROBERT F. BORNSTEIN" 0.4154 0.5794 0.0575 0.0727 0.0721 0.0151 0.00382 0.2235 0.0568 "Times-Roman" 0.0108 nil nil 0)
  ("In Zajonc's (1968) first experiment, subjects were presented" 0.0829 0.4812 0.0845 0.1008 0.1008 0.0163 0.00437 0.3046 0.0137 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("with a series of nonsense words at frequencies ranging from 0" 0.0675 0.4819 0.0987 0.1150 0.1150 0.0163 0.00437 0.2896 0.0297 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("to 25 exposures; they then rated each stimulus word for \"good-" 0.0679 0.4824 0.1126 0.1289 0.1289 0.0163 0.00437 0.3226 0.0130 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("ness of meaning\" (i.e., the extent to which the word connotes" 0.0683 0.4823 0.1268 0.1431 0.1431 0.0163 0.00442 0.3144 0.0277 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("\"good\" vs. \"bad\" affect) on a seven-point scale. Zajonc found" 0.0679 0.4812 0.1407 0.1570 0.1570 0.0163 0.00437 0.2444 0.0500 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("a positive relationship between number of exposures and the" 0.0675 0.4819 0.1545 0.1708 0.1708 0.0163 0.00437 0.2649 0.0078 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("average goodness rating for a word. He then replicated this" 0.0675 0.4823 0.1687 0.1850 0.1850 0.0163 0.00437 0.2442 0.0488 "Times-Roman" 0.0123 nil nil 0)
  ("study using a similar procedure but different stimuli: Chinese" 0.0675 0.4818 0.1829 0.1992 0.1992 0.0163 0.00437 0.3040 0.0359 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("ideographs were substituted for the nonsense words used in Ex-" 0.0679 0.4819 0.1971 0.2134 0.2134 0.0163 0.00437 0.3119 0.0716 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("periment 1. The results of Experiment 2 were consistent with" 0.0675 0.4809 0.2110 0.2273 0.2273 0.0163 0.00437 0.3319 0.0601 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("the findings of Zajonc's first study; again, rated goodness of" 0.0675 0.4820 0.2252 0.2415 0.2415 0.0163 0.00437 0.2330 0.0203 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("meaning was positively related to frequency of exposure." 0.0675 0.4383 0.2391 0.2554 0.2554 0.0163 0.00432 0.3196 0.0574 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("Zajonc's (1968) third experiment investigated the extent to" 0.0829 0.4818 0.2533 0.2696 0.2696 0.0163 0.00437 0.2770 0.0551 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("which typical exposure effects could be obtained with socially" 0.0671 0.4814 0.2671 0.2834 0.2834 0.0163 0.00437 0.2837 0.0401 "Times-Roman" 0.0123 nil nil 0)
  ("relevant stimuli. In this experiment, subjects were shown a se-" 0.0675 0.4815 0.2813 0.2976 0.2976 0.0163 0.00437 0.3481 0.0525 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("ries of faces (photographs of students taken from a college year-" 0.0675 0.4811 0.2952 0.3115 0.3115 0.0163 0.00437 0.2970 0.0236 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("book) at different exposure frequencies, after which they were" 0.0675 0.4818 0.3094 0.3257 0.3257 0.0163 0.00437 0.2810 0.0391 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("asked to make liking ratings of each stimulus person on a seven-" 0.0675 0.4811 0.3233 0.3396 0.3396 0.0163 0.00437 0.3767 0.0366 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("point scale. A significant, positive relationship between fre-" 0.0675 0.4815 0.3375 0.3538 0.3538 0.0163 0.00437 0.2791 0.0345 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("quency of exposure (25 presentations was the ceiling in this" 0.0675 0.4823 0.3514 0.3676 0.3676 0.0163 0.00429 0.2381 0.0480 "Times-Roman" 0.0123 nil nil 0)
  ("study) and mean liking rating of a stimulus was found." 0.0675 0.4228 0.3655 0.3818 0.3818 0.0163 0.00426 0.3827 0.0411 "Times-Roman" 0.0123 nil nil 0)
  ("Zajonc's (1968) fourth study was not concerned with the rela-" 0.0829 0.4819 0.3797 0.3960 0.3960 0.0163 0.0043 0.3521 0.0555 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("tionship between frequency of exposure and reported affect. In-" 0.0675 0.4815 0.3936 0.4099 0.4099 0.0163 0.00427 0.2953 0.0541 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("stead, Experiment 4 tested the hypothesis that repeated presen-" 0.0679 0.4819 0.4078 0.4241 0.4241 0.0163 0.00437 0.3005 0.0369 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("tations of a word will lead to a decrease in galvanic skin re-" 0.0675 0.4815 0.4217 0.4380 0.4380 0.0163 0.00438 0.2688 0.0438 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("sponse (GSR) fluctuations that result from stimulus exposures." 0.0679 0.4812 0.4359 0.4522 0.4522 0.0163 0.00437 0.3349 0.0437 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("Fifteen nonsense words served as stimuli and were presented" 0.0675 0.4808 0.4501 0.4664 0.4664 0.0163 0.00437 0.2684 0.0448 "Times-Roman" 0.0122 nil nil 0)
  ("from 1 to 25 times each in random order. Zajonc found a nega-" 0.0679 0.4819 0.4639 0.4802 0.4802 0.0163 0.00437 0.3458 0.0303 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("tive relationship between exposure frequency and mean GSR" 0.0671 0.4807 0.4781 0.4944 0.4944 0.0163 0.00437 0.2586 0.0234 "Times-Roman" 0.0122 nil nil 0)
  ("change in response to the final stimulus presentation, suggesting" 0.0675 0.4816 0.4920 0.5083 0.5083 0.0163 0.00439 0.3255 0.0459 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("that repeated, unreinforced exposure to words results in habit-" 0.0671 0.4815 0.5059 0.5222 0.5222 0.0163 0.00437 0.2739 0.0260 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("uation and in decreased autonomic arousal following later stim-" 0.0679 0.4815 0.5201 0.5364 0.5364 0.0163 0.00437 0.3383 0.0418 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("ulus presentations." 0.0675 0.1908 0.5343 0.5506 0.5506 0.0163 0.00437 0.2371 0.0285 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("Zajonc's (1968) monograph stimulated a great deal of inter-" 0.0829 0.4815 0.5482 0.5645 0.5645 0.0163 0.00437 0.3082 0.0551 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("est in mere exposure effects, and in the 20 years following the" 0.0671 0.4813 0.5621 0.5783 0.5783 0.0163 0.00425 0.2828 0.0182 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("publication of his seminal paper, over 130 articles reporting" 0.0675 0.4812 0.5762 0.5925 0.5925 0.0163 0.00437 0.2531 0.0737 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("more than 200 separate experiments assessing the exposure-" 0.0679 0.4820 0.5901 0.6064 0.6064 0.0163 0.00446 0.2558 0.0347 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("affect relationship appeared in the psychological literature. Re-" 0.0675 0.4815 0.6043 0.6206 0.6206 0.0163 0.00437 0.2639 0.0335 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("searchers investigating a wide range of topics—including adver-" 0.0675 0.4815 0.6182 0.6345 0.6345 0.0163 0.00432 0.3667 0.0605 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("tising effects (Sawyer, 1981), children's reactions to novel stim-" 0.0675 0.4811 0.6324 0.6487 0.6487 0.0163 0.00437 0.3129 0.0362 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("uli (Hutt, 1975), subliminal influences on behavior (Bornstein," 0.0679 0.4813 0.6463 0.6626 0.6626 0.0163 0.00435 0.3194 0.0166 "Times-Roman" 0.0121 nil nil 0)
  ("Leone, & Galley, 1987), social perceptions (Saegert, Swap, &" 0.0675 0.4809 0.6608 0.6771 0.6771 0.0163 0.00437 0.3019 0.0450 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("Zajonc, 1973), food preferences (Pliner, 1982), environmental" 0.0675 0.4808 0.6743 0.6906 0.6906 0.0163 0.00437 0.2672 0.0492 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("preferences (Herzog, Kaplan, & Kaplan, 1976), aesthetic judg-" 0.0675 0.4815 0.6885 0.7048 0.7048 0.0163 0.00437 0.3246 0.0745 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("ments (Berlyne, 1974), and verbal learning (Grush, 1976)—" 0.0679 0.4807 0.7027 0.7190 0.7190 0.0163 0.00437 0.2646 0.0402 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("have all used ideas and procedures from exposure effects re-" 0.0675 0.4811 0.7169 0.7332 0.7332 0.0163 0.00424 0.2053 0.0296 "Times-Roman" 0.0121 nil nil 0)
  ("search." 0.0675 0.1132 0.7308 0.7471 0.7471 0.0163 nil 0.1331 0.0457 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("Stang (1974b) reviewed selected methodological factors in-" 0.0829 0.4811 0.7450 0.7613 0.7613 0.0163 0.00437 0.2715 0.0366 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("fluencing the mere exposure effect in studies reported through" 0.0679 0.4809 0.7589 0.7752 0.7752 0.0163 0.00437 0.2697 0.0596 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("the early 1970s, and he concluded that a heterogeneous expo-" 0.0671 0.4811 0.7728 0.7890 0.7890 0.0163 0.00437 0.2049 0.0204 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("sure sequence and a delay between presentations and ratings" 0.0675 0.4814 0.7866 0.8029 0.8029 0.0163 0.00437 0.2154 0.0280 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("enhanced the effect. In addition, Stang found that auditory," 0.0671 0.4809 0.8008 0.8171 0.8171 0.0163 0.00437 0.2305 0.0629 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("painting, and pattern (i.e., matrice or line drawing) stimuli pro-" 0.0675 0.4811 0.8147 0.8310 0.8310 0.0163 0.00437 0.3936 0.0588 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("duced weaker exposure effects than did other types of stimuli." 0.0675 0.4812 0.8286 0.8449 0.8449 0.0163 0.00437 0.2815 0.0395 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("Harrison (1977) also reviewed selected areas of mere exposure" 0.0675 0.4809 0.8428 0.8591 0.8591 0.0163 0.00437 0.3048 0.0586 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("theory and research, concluding that stimulus complexity, a" 0.0675 0.4809 0.8567 0.8729 0.8729 0.0163 0.00437 0.2745 0.0427 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("heterogeneous presentation sequence, and delay between expo-" 0.0675 0.4811 0.8702 0.8865 0.8865 0.0163 0.00437 0.2363 0.0939 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("sures and ratings all enhanced the exposure-affect relationship." 0.0675 0.4812 0.8844 0.9007 0.9007 0.0163 0.00437 0.2671 0.0341 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("Unfortunately, neither Stang nor Harrison assessed the influ-" 0.5133 0.9277 0.0851 0.1014 0.1014 0.0163 0.00437 0.2586 0.0959 "Times-Roman" 0.0122 nil nil 0)
  ("ence of several important methodological variables (e.g., expo-" 0.5133 0.9278 0.0990 0.1153 0.1153 0.0163 0.00437 0.3031 0.0297 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("sure duration, maximum number of exposures) on the strength" 0.5133 0.9272 0.1129 0.1292 0.1292 0.0163 0.00437 0.3685 0.0278 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("of the exposure effect. Neither review assessed the influence of" 0.5129 0.9278 0.1271 0.1434 0.1434 0.0163 0.00428 0.2823 0.0135 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("subject variables (e.g., age of subject, personality and individual" 0.5133 0.9270 0.1410 0.1573 0.1573 0.0163 0.00437 0.3058 0.0455 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("difference variables) on the exposure-affect relationship. Fur-" 0.5125 0.9273 0.1552 0.1714 0.1714 0.0163 0.00437 0.2268 0.0634 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("thermore, neither review systematically assessed the influence" 0.5129 0.9275 0.1690 0.1853 0.1853 0.0163 0.00437 0.2926 0.0644 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("of different parameters." 0.5129 0.6675 0.1829 0.1992 0.1992 0.0163 0.00437 0.3076 0.0135 "Times-Roman" 0.0123 nil nil 0)
  ("A number of models have been proposed to account for the" 0.5283 0.9273 0.1971 0.2134 0.2134 0.0163 0.00437 0.2784 0.0111 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("production of mere exposure effects, and reviews of empirical" 0.5125 0.9270 0.2110 0.2273 0.2273 0.0163 0.00437 0.3059 0.0719 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("support for these theoretical models by Harrison (1977) and" 0.5129 0.9266 0.2252 0.2415 0.2415 0.0163 0.00437 0.2499 0.0505 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("Stang (1974b) produced inconsistent and inconclusive results." 0.5129 0.9266 0.2394 0.2557 0.2557 0.0163 0.00437 0.2345 0.0366 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("In part, these inconsistencies were due to unsystematic sam-" 0.5129 0.9278 0.2533 0.2696 0.2696 0.0163 0.00437 0.2847 0.0138 "Times-Roman" 0.0129 nil nil 0)
  ("pling of the mere exposure literature. Because certain critical" 0.5125 0.9266 0.2675 0.2837 0.2837 0.0163 0.00437 0.2709 0.0337 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("methodological and subject variables were not assessed in these" 0.5129 0.9272 0.2816 0.2979 0.2979 0.0163 0.00437 0.2976 0.0994 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("reviews, predictions made by different theoretical models have" 0.5125 0.9272 0.2955 0.3118 0.3118 0.0163 0.00437 0.3100 0.0534 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("not been adequately tested against each other. Thus, no single" 0.5133 0.9276 0.3094 0.3257 0.3257 0.0163 0.00437 0.2460 0.0213 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("model of exposure effects has clearly emerged as one that is best" 0.5129 0.9270 0.3236 0.3399 0.3399 0.0163 0.00433 0.3389 0.0412 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("supported by the range of empirical findings in this area (see" 0.5129 0.9272 0.3375 0.3538 0.3538 0.0163 0.00437 0.2645 0.0643 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("Harrison, 1977; Sawyer, 1981; Vanbeseleare, 1983). Many key" 0.5133 0.9268 0.3517 0.3680 0.3680 0.0163 0.00429 0.3292 0.0619 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("variables influencing the exposure effect are best assessed using" 0.5129 0.9274 0.3655 0.3818 0.3818 0.0163 0.00437 0.2505 0.0588 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("meta-analytic techniques (Rosenthal, 1984). Meta-analysis per-" 0.5133 0.9273 0.3797 0.3960 0.3960 0.0163 0.00437 0.3369 0.0901 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("mits a systematic assessment of the influence of each critical" 0.5129 0.9270 0.3936 0.4099 0.4099 0.0163 0.00435 0.2920 0.0294 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("variable on the size of the exposure effect and is therefore the" 0.5129 0.9272 0.4078 0.4241 0.4241 0.0163 0.00426 0.2365 0.0529 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("most appropriate technique for assessing the fit of different the-" 0.5129 0.9273 0.4220 0.4383 0.4383 0.0163 0.0043 0.3032 0.0323 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("oretical models of the exposure effect with the results of rele-" 0.5129 0.9278 0.4359 0.4522 0.4522 0.0163 0.00437 0.2825 0.0481 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("vant empirical studies." 0.5129 0.6621 0.4498 0.4661 0.4661 0.0163 0.00437 0.3155 0.0279 "Times-Roman" 0.0122 nil nil 0)
  ("Advantages, limitations, and potential problems in the use of" 0.5279 0.9270 0.4639 0.4802 0.4802 0.0163 0.00436 0.3498 0.0792 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("meta-analysis as a technique for assessing the empirical litera-" 0.5125 0.9269 0.4778 0.4941 0.4941 0.0163 0.00437 0.3030 0.0906 "Times-Roman" 0.0127 nil nil 0)
  ("ture on a topic have been discussed elsewhere (Booth-Kewley" 0.5125 0.9264 0.4917 0.5080 0.5080 0.0163 0.00437 0.2693 0.0272 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("& Friedman, 1987; Light & Kllemer, 1984; Rosenthal, 1984)." 0.5125 0.9272 0.5059 0.5222 0.5222 0.0163 0.00437 0.3463 0.0128 "Times-Roman" 0.0122 nil nil 0)
  ("Generally, criticisms of meta-analysis fall into four areas: (a)" 0.5125 0.9274 0.5201 0.5364 0.5364 0.0163 0.00437 0.2984 0.0674 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("sampling bias with respect to experiments included in the meta-" 0.5125 0.9265 0.5340 0.5503 0.5503 0.0163 0.00437 0.3905 0.0608 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("analysis (including failure to sample unpublished, nonsignifi-" 0.5125 0.9269 0.5482 0.5645 0.5645 0.0163 0.00437 0.2724 0.0523 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("cant findings); (b) variability in the strength and methodological" 0.5121 0.9262 0.5621 0.5783 0.5783 0.0163 0.00434 0.3408 0.0278 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("soundness of different experiments; (c) averaging effect sizes" 0.5125 0.9273 0.5762 0.5925 0.5925 0.0163 0.00419 0.2437 0.0674 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("across studies that vary markedly with respect to methodolo-" 0.5125 0.9274 0.5901 0.6064 0.6064 0.0163 0.00437 0.3002 0.0415 "Times-Roman" 0.0129 nil nil 0)
  ("gies, variables assessed, characteristics of subjects, selection" 0.5121 0.9259 0.6043 0.6206 0.6206 0.0163 0.00437 0.2468 0.0288 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("procedures, and so on (i.e., combining \"apples and oranges\";" 0.5125 0.9274 0.6182 0.6345 0.6345 0.0163 0.00443 0.2532 0.0769 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("see Glass, 1978); and (d) attempting to summarize a broad," 0.5125 0.9267 0.6321 0.6484 0.6484 0.0163 0.00437 0.3009 0.0214 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("complex issue or topic with a few overall effect size estimates." 0.5125 0.9262 0.6463 0.6626 0.6626 0.0163 0.00437 0.3320 0.0565 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("The nature of the exposure effects literature and the questions" 0.5125 0.9269 0.6605 0.6767 0.6767 0.0163 0.00432 0.2491 0.0261 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("being addressed in this review are such that meta-analysis of" 0.5125 0.9266 0.6743 0.6906 0.6906 0.0163 0.00437 0.2669 0.0362 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("research on the exposure-affect relationship has many advan-" 0.5121 0.9265 0.6885 0.7048 0.7048 0.0163 0.00437 0.2522 0.0539 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("tages and none of these disadvantages." 0.5121 0.7621 0.7027 0.7190 0.7190 0.0163 0.00437 0.2597 0.0330 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("Meta-analytic techniques are particularly suited to an assess-" 0.5275 0.9265 0.7166 0.7329 0.7329 0.0163 0.00442 0.3213 0.0918 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("ment of the exposure effects literature for several reasons. First," 0.5125 0.9263 0.7305 0.7468 0.7468 0.0163 0.00437 0.2897 0.0337 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("the majority of studies conducted in this area after the publica-" 0.5121 0.9265 0.7447 0.7610 0.7610 0.0163 0.00437 0.3111 0.0204 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("tion of Zajonc's (1968) monograph used very similar methodol-" 0.5121 0.9265 0.7586 0.7749 0.7749 0.0163 0.00437 0.3555 0.0260 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("ogies (derived from Zajonc's methods and procedures), so that" 0.5121 0.9258 0.7724 0.7887 0.7887 0.0163 0.00437 0.2885 0.0332 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("comparison of results across studies is not unduly influenced" 0.5121 0.9262 0.7863 0.8026 0.8026 0.0163 0.00437 0.2483 0.0763 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("by researchers' using a wide variety of methodologies to assess" 0.5121 0.9269 0.8002 0.8165 0.8165 0.0163 0.00438 0.3038 0.0162 "Times-Roman" 0.0129 nil nil 0)
  ("the exposure-affect relationship (Criticisms b and c, noted ear-" 0.5121 0.9269 0.8144 0.8307 0.8307 0.0163 0.00437 0.3090 0.0204 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("lier). The limited range of dependent measures, stimuli, and ex-" 0.5125 0.9269 0.8283 0.8446 0.8446 0.0163 0.00437 0.3649 0.0304 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("posure paradigms used in these experiments help to reduce be-" 0.5121 0.9265 0.8421 0.8584 0.8584 0.0163 0.00437 0.3048 0.0447 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("tween-studies variance (Criticism c). Because methodological" 0.5125 0.9262 0.8560 0.8723 0.8723 0.0163 0.00437 0.3050 0.0893 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("and subject variables can be classified into discrete categories," 0.5121 0.9267 0.8699 0.8862 0.8862 0.0163 0.00441 0.2497 0.0239 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("and because separate effect size estimates can be calculated" 0.5125 0.9266 0.8838 0.9001 0.9001 0.0163 0.00437 0.2274 0.0239 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0))
  (3
  ("EXPOSURE AND AFFECT" 0.4113 0.5908 0.0572 0.0718 0.0718 0.0146 0.00395 0.1608 0.0779 "Times-Roman" 0.0112 nil nil 0)
  ("267" 0.9046 0.9317 0.0555 0.0718 0.0718 0.0163 nil 0.0000 0.0271 "Times-Roman" 0.0130 nil nil 0)
  ("within each category of a particular variable, the influence of" 0.0696 0.4869 0.0839 0.1002 0.1002 0.0163 0.00441 0.2538 0.0430 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("specific variables on the exposure-affect relationship can be as-" 0.0700 0.4857 0.0984 0.1147 0.1147 0.0163 0.00441 0.2663 0.0481 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("sessed (Criticism d). Many critical variables influencing the ex-" 0.0700 0.4857 0.1123 0.1286 0.1286 0.0163 0.0044 0.3472 0.0390 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("posure effect (e.g., age of subject, exposure duration, maximum" 0.0704 0.4855 0.1265 0.1428 0.1428 0.0163 0.00441 0.3516 0.0441 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("number of stimulus exposures) typically vary between, rather" 0.0704 0.4849 0.1404 0.1566 0.1566 0.0163 0.00441 0.2968 0.0523 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("than within, studies, so that systematic assessments of these" 0.0700 0.4859 0.1542 0.1705 0.1705 0.0163 0.00441 0.2994 0.0299 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("variables can only be performed by combining the results of" 0.0704 0.4857 0.1684 0.1847 0.1847 0.0163 0.00434 0.2684 0.0586 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("different experiments via meta-analytic techniques. For many" 0.0700 0.4851 0.1823 0.1986 0.1986 0.0163 0.00441 0.3163 0.0541 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("variables (e.g., exposure duration, delay between exposures and" 0.0704 0.4849 0.1965 0.2128 0.2128 0.0163 0.00441 0.2932 0.0586 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("ratings), a traditional comparison of the results of individual" 0.0708 0.4854 0.2104 0.2267 0.2267 0.0163 0.00441 0.2689 0.0537 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("studies produces conflicting results, whereas meta-analysis of" 0.0704 0.4857 0.2246 0.2409 0.2409 0.0163 0.00441 0.2759 0.0456 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("all studies classified into salient dimensions for the variable in" 0.0704 0.4855 0.2388 0.2550 0.2550 0.0163 0.00441 0.2939 0.0165 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("question produces clearer and more consistent findings (see" 0.0704 0.4863 0.2530 0.2692 0.2692 0.0163 0.00441 0.2304 0.0552 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("Light & Pillemer [1984], Rosenthal [1984], and Masters, Can-" 0.0712 0.4861 0.2665 0.2828 0.2828 0.0163 0.00441 0.3571 0.0354 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("tor, & Hedges [1988] for discussions of the value of meta-ana-" 0.0704 0.4861 0.2807 0.2970 0.2970 0.0163 0.00434 0.3167 0.0221 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("lytic techniques in such circumstances). Finally, a fail-safe N" 0.0708 0.4859 0.2949 0.3112 0.3112 0.0163 0.00441 0.2723 0.0289 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("(Rosenthal, 1979, 1984) can be calculated for each effect size" 0.0704 0.4859 0.3088 0.3251 0.3251 0.0163 0.00423 0.2313 0.0763 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("estimate in the meta-analysis to assess the possible influence of" 0.0704 0.4857 0.3230 0.3393 0.3393 0.0163 0.0044 0.3126 0.0551 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("sampling bias (i.e., underestimate of unpublished, nonsignifi-" 0.0704 0.4861 0.3369 0.3532 0.3532 0.0163 0.00441 0.3026 0.0614 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("cant findings) on effect sizes (Criticism a)." 0.0704 0.3454 0.3510 0.3673 0.3673 0.0163 0.00436 0.3385 0.0280 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("The purpose of this article is to examine systematically the" 0.0854 0.4859 0.3649 0.3812 0.3812 0.0163 0.00441 0.3217 0.0265 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("influence of methodological and subject variables on the expo-" 0.0704 0.4861 0.3791 0.3954 0.3954 0.0163 0.0044 0.2693 0.0605 "Times-Roman" 0.0123 nil nil 0)
  ("sure-affect relationship, in order to assess support for different" 0.0708 0.4858 0.3930 0.4093 0.4093 0.0163 0.00441 0.2244 0.0698 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("theoretical models of the exposure effect. The analysis involves" 0.0704 0.4869 0.4072 0.4235 0.4235 0.0163 0.00441 0.3054 0.0692 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("several steps. First, studies of the exposure-affect relationship" 0.0708 0.4860 0.4211 0.4374 0.4374 0.0163 0.00441 0.2328 0.0445 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("that appeared in the first two decades following publication of" 0.0708 0.4857 0.4353 0.4516 0.4516 0.0163 0.00434 0.2783 0.0259 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("Zajonc's (1968) monograph are reviewed. The influence of each" 0.0708 0.4892 0.4491 0.4654 0.4654 0.0163 0.0043 0.3288 0.0560 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("methodological and subject variable on the exposure effect is" 0.0708 0.4864 0.4633 0.4796 0.4796 0.0163 0.00429 0.2452 0.1003 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("assessed using meta-analytic techniques (Rosenthal, 1984). For" 0.0708 0.4857 0.4775 0.4938 0.4938 0.0163 0.00441 0.2992 0.0518 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("reasons that are discussed later, certain variables (e.g., stimulus" 0.0712 0.4864 0.4914 0.5077 0.5077 0.0163 0.00441 0.3026 0.0485 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("complexity) could not be subjected to meta-analysis. However," 0.0712 0.4862 0.5053 0.5216 0.5216 0.0163 0.0044 0.3120 0.0795 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("these variables are discussed in the following sections as they" 0.0708 0.4855 0.5192 0.5355 0.5355 0.0163 0.00433 0.2427 0.0337 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("apply to particular issues. The implications of this review and" 0.0708 0.4854 0.5334 0.5496 0.5496 0.0163 0.00439 0.3059 0.0367 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("analysis are discussed in the context of theoretical models of" 0.0712 0.4865 0.5475 0.5638 0.5638 0.0163 0.00438 0.2484 0.0523 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("mere exposure effects. Empirical support for these models is" 0.0712 0.4864 0.5617 0.5780 0.5780 0.0163 0.00441 0.2960 0.0334 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("assessed, and modifications in the best-supported model of ex-" 0.0712 0.4865 0.5756 0.5919 0.5919 0.0163 0.00435 0.3066 0.0580 "Times-Roman" 0.0129 nil nil 0)
  ("posure effects are described, which allow the model to better" 0.0712 0.4861 0.5898 0.6061 0.6061 0.0163 0.00438 0.2861 0.0448 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("accommodate the range of findings in this area. An evolution-" 0.0708 0.4865 0.6037 0.6200 0.6200 0.0163 0.0044 0.3305 0.0912 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("ary basis of the exposure effect is briefly discussed in the context" 0.0712 0.4858 0.6176 0.6339 0.6339 0.0163 0.00429 0.3003 0.0211 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("of findings from this review." 0.0717 0.2541 0.6318 0.6481 0.6481 0.0163 0.00419 0.3388 0.0135 "Times-Roman" 0.0123 nil nil 0)
  ("Meta-Analysis" 0.2258 0.3323 0.6653 0.6833 0.6833 0.0180 nil 0.4141 0.1065 "Times-Roman" 0.0139 nil nil 0)
  ("Inclusion Criteria" 0.0717 0.2026 0.6919 0.7084 0.7084 0.0165 0.00445 0.2746 0.0680 "Times-Italic" 0.0135 nil t 0)
  ("This review and meta-analysis of the exposure-affect rela-" 0.0871 0.4869 0.7160 0.7323 0.7323 0.0163 0.00434 0.2564 0.0300 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("tionship includes studies of unreinforced exposure and affect" 0.0712 0.4866 0.7299 0.7462 0.7462 0.0163 0.00428 0.2132 0.0546 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("that were published between 1968 and 1987. The review in-" 0.0712 0.4865 0.7441 0.7603 0.7603 0.0163 0.0043 0.2504 0.0262 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("cludes only articles published after Zajonc's (1968) monograph" 0.0717 0.4859 0.7579 0.7742 0.7742 0.0163 0.00441 0.3104 0.0419 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("for several reasons. First, studies in this area conducted prior to" 0.0717 0.4864 0.7721 0.7884 0.7884 0.0163 0.00441 0.2746 0.0172 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("the mid-1960s were often methodologically unsound (see Za-" 0.0717 0.4869 0.7863 0.8026 0.8026 0.0163 0.00441 0.2824 0.0201 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("jonc, 1968). Many early studies used improper control groups" 0.0700 0.4869 0.8002 0.8165 0.8165 0.0163 0.00441 0.3125 0.0332 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("or conditions, or no controls at all, making it impossible to de-" 0.0717 0.4869 0.8144 0.8307 0.8307 0.0163 0.00447 0.3434 0.0142 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("rive effect size estimates for meta-analysis. With the publication" 0.0721 0.4863 0.8280 0.8442 0.8442 0.0163 0.00441 0.3952 0.0253 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("of Zajonc's seminal paper and his description of experimental" 0.0712 0.4862 0.8421 0.8584 0.8584 0.0163 0.00441 0.3010 0.0139 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("approaches to the study of exposure effects, the methodologies" 0.0717 0.4873 0.8563 0.8726 0.8726 0.0163 0.00438 0.2707 0.0741 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("used in these studies have improved markedly and have become" 0.0721 0.4863 0.8699 0.8862 0.8862 0.0163 0.00441 0.3485 0.0300 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("much more consistent across experiments. Limiting this review" 0.0717 0.4860 0.8838 0.9001 0.9001 0.0163 0.00441 0.3649 0.0372 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("to papers published since 1968 allows for discussion of individ-" 0.5162 0.9319 0.0839 0.1002 0.1002 0.0163 0.00441 0.2954 0.0130 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("ual experiments; if the many pre-1968 studies were included, it" 0.5167 0.9321 0.0981 0.1144 0.1144 0.0163 0.00441 0.3461 0.0206 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("would simply become unwieldy owing to the large number of" 0.5167 0.9320 0.1120 0.1283 0.1283 0.0163 0.00441 0.3378 0.0404 "Times-Roman" 0.0123 nil nil 0)
  ("papers that have little value concerning the theoretical and em-" 0.5171 0.9328 0.1262 0.1424 0.1424 0.0163 0.00441 0.3029 0.0429 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("pirical issues raised in this review. Stang (1974b) reviewed stud-" 0.5167 0.9319 0.1401 0.1563 0.1563 0.0163 0.00441 0.3322 0.0423 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("ies in this area that were published prior to 1968." 0.5171 0.8387 0.1542 0.1705 0.1705 0.0163 0.00441 0.3296 0.0185 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("This review includes only experiments that investigate affect" 0.5325 0.9321 0.1681 0.1844 0.1844 0.0163 0.00441 0.3265 0.0300 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("changes in response to mere (i.e., unreinforced) exposure to vi-" 0.5167 0.9324 0.1823 0.1986 0.1986 0.0163 0.00441 0.3177 0.0523 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("sual and auditory stimuli. The vast majority of mere exposure" 0.5171 0.9326 0.1962 0.2125 0.2125 0.0163 0.00441 0.3318 0.0264 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("experiments have studied attitudes toward these stimuli. Stud-" 0.5171 0.9324 0.2104 0.2267 0.2267 0.0163 0.00441 0.3274 0.0811 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("ies that involve exposure to other types of stimuli (i.e., olfactory" 0.5171 0.9322 0.2246 0.2409 0.2409 0.0163 0.00441 0.3620 0.0184 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("and gustatory) present a different set of methodological issues" 0.5171 0.9327 0.2384 0.2547 0.2547 0.0163 0.00441 0.2562 0.0240 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("that are only tangentially related to findings obtained using bet-" 0.5171 0.9328 0.2526 0.2689 0.2689 0.0163 0.00441 0.3238 0.0262 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("ter-controlled visual and auditory stimuli. Certain key variables" 0.5171 0.9327 0.2665 0.2828 0.2828 0.0163 0.00441 0.3375 0.0891 "Times-Roman" 0.0127 nil nil 0)
  ("(e.g., exposure duration, stimulus complexity) are difficult to" 0.5171 0.9323 0.2804 0.2967 0.2967 0.0163 0.00441 0.3135 0.0315 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("measure in olfactory and gustatory stimuli, making interexperi-" 0.5175 0.9328 0.2946 0.3109 0.3109 0.0163 0.00441 0.3826 0.0558 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("ment comparisons difficult. In addition, exposure to certain" 0.5175 0.9313 0.3088 0.3251 0.3251 0.0163 0.00441 0.2862 0.0337 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("gustatory and olfactory stimuli may be reinforcing in and of" 0.5167 0.9316 0.3227 0.3390 0.3390 0.0163 0.0043 0.2805 0.0619 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("itself (see, e.g., C. S. Crandall's, 1984, study of exposure effects" 0.5171 0.9326 0.3369 0.3532 0.3532 0.0163 0.00441 0.3294 0.0316 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("with doughnuts), so that such experiments can no longer be" 0.5171 0.9322 0.3507 0.3670 0.3670 0.0163 0.00441 0.2521 0.0293 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("strictly classified as mere exposure research. Although these" 0.5171 0.9322 0.3649 0.3812 0.3812 0.0163 0.00441 0.2672 0.0451 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("studies are not included in the meta-analysis, they will be dis-" 0.5171 0.9328 0.3788 0.3951 0.3951 0.0163 0.00441 0.3091 0.0449 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("cussed in later sections of this article." 0.5171 0.7613 0.3930 0.4093 0.4093 0.0163 0.00441 0.2784 0.0424 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("Studies that include any type of reinforcement introduced" 0.5329 0.9324 0.4072 0.4235 0.4235 0.0163 0.00441 0.2520 0.0481 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("during the exposure or rating phases of the experiment are not" 0.5171 0.9320 0.4211 0.4374 0.4374 0.0163 0.00442 0.2958 0.0449 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("included in the meta-analysis, to avoid contaminating the anal-" 0.5175 0.9328 0.4353 0.4516 0.4516 0.0163 0.00442 0.3356 0.0570 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("ysis with experiments investigating phenomena other than un-" 0.5175 0.9328 0.4491 0.4654 0.4654 0.0163 0.00441 0.3247 0.0243 "Times-Roman" 0.0123 nil nil 0)
  ("reinforced stimulus exposure. A number of experiments inves-" 0.5171 0.9323 0.4633 0.4796 0.4796 0.0163 0.00441 0.3421 0.0675 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("tigating familiarity effects in social settings (e.g., Ebbeson, Kjos," 0.5171 0.9325 0.4775 0.4938 0.4938 0.0163 0.00441 0.3393 0.0503 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("& Konecni, 1976; Segal, 1974) were eliminated on the basis of" 0.5175 0.9330 0.4914 0.5077 0.5077 0.0163 0.00441 0.3426 0.0132 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("this criterion, because it was not clear to what extent attitude" 0.5175 0.9326 0.5053 0.5216 0.5216 0.0163 0.00441 0.2972 0.0248 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("changes in these studies were induced by mere exposure or by" 0.5171 0.9322 0.5195 0.5358 0.5358 0.0163 0.00441 0.2745 0.0523 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("reinforcement derived from repeated social interaction." 0.5175 0.8829 0.5334 0.5496 0.5496 0.0163 0.00441 0.2859 0.0930 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("In addition, the numerous studies of the relationship between" 0.5333 0.9322 0.5472 0.5635 0.5635 0.0163 0.00441 0.3482 0.0135 "Times-Roman" 0.0123 nil nil 0)
  ("novelty-familiarity and infant attention/looking time that fol-" 0.5175 0.9328 0.5614 0.5777 0.5777 0.0163 0.00441 0.3129 0.1260 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("lowed Fantz's (1964) experiment (e.g., Balogh & Porter, 1986;" 0.5175 0.9328 0.5753 0.5916 0.5916 0.0163 0.00441 0.3287 0.0379 "Times-Roman" 0.0123 nil nil 0)
  ("Faw & Nunnally, 1968; D. Greenberg, Uzgiris, & Hunt, 1970;" 0.5179 0.9332 0.5898 0.6061 0.6061 0.0163 0.00441 0.3740 0.0266 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("Hunter, Ames, & Koopman, 1983; Hunter, Ross, & Ames," 0.5175 0.9321 0.6034 0.6197 0.6197 0.0163 0.00441 0.3409 0.0504 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("1982; Nachmann, Stern, & Best, 1986) are not included in the" 0.5196 0.9326 0.6176 0.6339 0.6339 0.0163 0.00437 0.3385 0.0361 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("meta-analysis. The relationship between attention and affect" 0.5179 0.9325 0.6318 0.6481 0.6481 0.0163 0.00441 0.2792 0.0920 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("may vary as a function of experimental paradigm and age of" 0.5179 0.9324 0.6456 0.6619 0.6619 0.0163 0.00429 0.2762 0.0274 "Times-Roman" 0.0122 nil nil 0)
  ("subject (see D. Greenberg et al., 1970; Nachmann et al., 1986;" 0.5175 0.9332 0.6598 0.6761 0.6761 0.0163 0.00443 0.3263 0.0459 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("Wetherford & Cohen, 1973), and research suggests that both" 0.5179 0.9326 0.6740 0.6903 0.6903 0.0163 0.00441 0.2967 0.0738 "Times-Roman" 0.0123 nil nil 0)
  ("pleasant stimuli and extremely unpleasant stimuli (e.g., depic-" 0.5179 0.9332 0.6879 0.7042 0.7042 0.0163 0.00441 0.3448 0.0539 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("tions of violent acts) are associated with increased attention and" 0.5175 0.9320 0.7021 0.7184 0.7184 0.0163 0.00441 0.3223 0.0322 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("scrutiny (Scrivner & Safer, 1988). Thus, treating visual prefer-" 0.5179 0.9327 0.7160 0.7323 0.7323 0.0163 0.00441 0.2880 0.0542 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("ence or fixation time as an affect measure comparable to those" 0.5179 0.9330 0.7302 0.7465 0.7465 0.0163 0.00438 0.3240 0.0301 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("obtained via verbal report is unwarranted. Studies of novelty-" 0.5179 0.9324 0.7441 0.7603 0.7603 0.0163 0.00441 0.2777 0.0564 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("familiarity and infant attention are discussed later, in the con-" 0.5179 0.9328 0.7582 0.7745 0.7745 0.0163 0.00434 0.2746 0.0694 "Times-Roman" 0.0122 nil nil 0)
  ("text of findings from exposure effects research." 0.5179 0.8246 0.7721 0.7884 0.7884 0.0163 0.0042 0.2791 0.0250 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("Finally, the meta-analysis includes only studies that used hu-" 0.5337 0.9332 0.7863 0.8026 0.8026 0.0163 0.00441 0.3074 0.0492 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("man subjects. A number of investigations in this area have used" 0.5179 0.9320 0.8002 0.8165 0.8165 0.0163 0.00441 0.3625 0.0284 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("infrahuman subjects (e.g., Navarick & Strouthes, 1969;" 0.5179 0.9332 0.8141 0.8304 0.8304 0.0163 0.00441 0.3477 0.0773 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("Strouthes, 1971); these experiments were reviewed by Hill" 0.5183 0.9328 0.8283 0.8446 0.8446 0.0163 0.00441 0.2843 0.0670 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0)
  ("(1978), who found strong support for the existence of exposure" 0.5175 0.9330 0.8421 0.8584 0.8584 0.0163 0.00427 0.2820 0.0479 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("effects in infrahumans. However, the methodologies, stimuli," 0.5179 0.9325 0.8560 0.8723 0.8723 0.0163 0.00422 0.3217 0.0394 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0)
  ("and dependent measures used in these studies differ so signifi-" 0.5179 0.9335 0.8702 0.8865 0.8865 0.0163 0.00441 0.2594 0.0239 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0)
  ("cantly from those used in exposure effects research with human" 0.5175 0.9322 0.8841 0.9004 0.9004 0.0163 0.00441 0.3462 0.0405 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0))
  (4
  ("268" 0.0704 0.0977 0.0525 0.0722 0.0722 0.0197 nil 0.0000 0.0273 "Times-Roman" 0.0144 nil nil 0)
  ("ROBERT F. BORNSTEIN" 0.4175 0.5815 0.0566 0.0711 0.0711 0.0146 0.00391 0.2449 0.0566 "Times-Roman" 0.0109 nil nil 0)
